  "response": "When the designer and the owner each attribute a bridge collapse to the other, the question is settled by physical and documentary evidence rather than by the competing narratives of their experts. Forensic engineers recover the fracture surfaces of failed members for metallurgical examination, and that recovery in turn yields a fractographic analysis that distinguishes fatigue striations from ductile overload features on the broken steel. That discrimination carries evidentiary weight: the striation pattern on the recovered steel shows that pre-existing fatigue cracks were present in the fractured gusset plates. On this point the possibilities are mutually exclusive \u2014 either such cracks had already formed in those plates before the structure came down, or no pre-existing fatigue cracks were present in the fractured gusset plates, and the one holds only if the other does not.\n\nThe design side of the inquiry is conducted on paper. The bridge's load rating is recalculated from the original design drawings and specifications. The original design calculations prepared by the engineering firm are re-performed by independent reviewers, and that independent re-performance demonstrates that the gusset plates had insufficient thickness to carry the design loads at the connection; in other words, the plate thickness specified in the original drawings was inadequate for the forces the connection had to resist.\n\nWhereas the design question is pursued through the drawings and the recalculated rating, the maintenance question is pursued through the owner's paper trail. Investigators subpoena the owner's inspection and repair records to establish the corrosion history of the structure, a task that occupies much of the early work. Federal regulations require routine inspection of highway bridges every six months. The documentary record is gathered at the outset of the investigation; the physical quantification of deterioration on the structure itself comes only afterward. Section loss measurements quantify the reduction in member capacity caused by corrosion, which is routine field work on a wrecked span. Corrosion-induced section loss in the gusset plates reduced them below the thickness called for in the original design. The finding that the thickness specified in the drawings was already insufficient for the connection's forces cannot be squared with an account in which corrosion loss alone carried the plates below their designed dimension; both cannot describe the same connection.\n\nThe arithmetic of the failure nonetheless permits one firm conclusion, and it frames the allocation of fault: at least one of two conditions must have obtained, and perhaps both held together \u2014 the loads acting on the bridge at the time of collapse exceeded the loads anticipated in the original design, or the capacity of the failed connection was less than the capacity the original design required of it. Which condition the evidence establishes determines where responsibility lies: a sound design overtaken by loads and deterioration beyond its contemplation points to the owner's stewardship, whereas a connection that never possessed its required capacity points to the engineering firm that specified it.",
